<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!DOCTYPE PLUGIN [
<!ENTITY name      "unraid-disk-event-trigger">
<!ENTITY author    "maxandcheeses">
<!ENTITY version   "2026.08.03.28">
<!ENTITY pluginURL "https://github.com/maxandcheeses/unraid-disk-event-trigger/raw/main/unraid-disk-event-trigger.plg">
<!ENTITY srcURL     "https://github.com/maxandcheeses/unraid-disk-event-trigger/releases/download/v&version;/unraid-disk-event-trigger.txz">
<!ENTITY srcMD5     "dab8ef77edca623a748d1ac390623805">
]>

<PLUGIN name="&name;" author="&author;" version="&version;" pluginURL="&pluginURL;" launch="Settings/unraid-disk-event-trigger" icon="images/icon.png" min="6.9">

<CHANGES>
###2026.08.03.28
- **CHANGE**: disk temperature is now read exclusively from Unraid's own cached value in disks.ini - the plugin no longer shells out to `smartctl` itself as a fallback. `smartctl -A` was slow enough to be a poor fit for a poller running every poll_interval seconds. Trade-off: if nothing has refreshed a disk's temp recently (e.g. no one has the Main webGUI page open), a temp-based condition can read as unavailable until Unraid's own background refresh updates disks.ini again - array/data rebuild, parity check, and usage-based conditions are unaffected, since they never used smartctl.

###2026.08.03.27
- Add: clicking a rule's "Fired"/"Idle" status badge now clears its fired flag (only does anything when it's currently Fired) - a quick manual reset without needing "Check Device State" or waiting for the condition to clear on its own. The rule re-fires next poll cycle if its condition is still met.

###2026.08.03.26
- Add: **Next poll cycle** countdown under Service, so you can see at a glance when the poller will next check conditions instead of guessing from the configured interval.
- Add: a rule can now name other rules to **also reset** when it fires (transitions from not-fired to fired) - e.g. an ON rule resetting its paired OFF rule's fired flag, so hysteresis pairs don't get stuck. Only happens on that transition, not on every force-resend re-assertion.
- Change: reworded each condition's "Turn ON/Turn OFF" dropdown to "Met when value is &#8805;/&#8804; threshold" (and "Met while active/inactive" for parity check/rebuild) - the old "Turn ON/OFF" wording was left over from before conditions and actions were split apart and no longer made sense on a condition, which doesn't turn anything on or off itself.

###2026.08.03.25
- Add: individual conditions within a rule can now be folded/unfolded (like rules and connections already could), showing a one-line summary (e.g. "Disk temp &#8805; 40&#176;C") when collapsed - useful once a rule has several conditions.
- Change: **Test** and **Check Device State** on a rule now show their result inline in the rule editor instead of a popup alert, matching how Test Conditions and the global Connections' Test button already behave - the whole settings page now reports test/status results the same way everywhere.

###2026.08.03.24
- Fix: **Test Conditions** (and Save &amp; Apply's confirmation message) silently did nothing when a global Connection happened to share the same position/index as the rule being tested - both rules and connections use the same `.rule-box[data-index]` markup, so the unscoped lookup could grab a Connection's box instead of the Rule's and find no results panel to write into. Scoped both lookups to `#htt-rules` so they always find the right box.

###2026.08.03.23
- **CHANGE**: removed the rule-level "Action" (Turn ON/Turn OFF) dropdown - it was a confusing extra step on top of each protocol's own config, and only Tasmota HTTP actually needed an on/off choice (MQTT and webhook already fully describe what they send via their topic+payload / URL+body). Tasmota HTTP rules now pick "Command: Power On/Power Off" directly within the HTTP section instead; existing rules migrate their old Action choice into that field automatically. The rule status badge is now a simple "Fired"/"Idle" instead of "Fired: ON/OFF" since there's no single rule-level polarity to label anymore.

###2026.08.03.22
- Add: each rule now shows its own **Save &amp; Apply** button next to the reorder arrows whenever it has unsaved edits, and hides it again once saved (or once it matches what's on disk) - a quicker way to spot and save a changed rule than scrolling to the bottom of its editor.
- **CHANGE**: adding, removing, duplicating, or reordering a rule no longer saves immediately - a "Save &amp; Apply (adding/removing/reordering rules)" button now appears next to "+ Add Rule" and must be clicked for those structural changes to actually take effect, matching how field edits already require an explicit Save.

###2026.08.03.21
- Add: **Test Conditions** button on each rule, which evaluates its conditions right now against live disk/array state (no command sent) and shows a per-condition breakdown - each condition's current reading, whether it's met (&#10003;/&#10007;), its AND/OR join with the running result, and the overall true/false outcome - so it's easy to see exactly which condition is stopping the rule from firing.
- Add: **Duplicate condition** button on each condition, to quickly clone one (e.g. as a starting point for a second threshold on the same metric, or a similar condition on different disks).

###2026.08.03.20
- Add: each condition after the first in a rule can now be joined with AND or OR (evaluated left-to-right, no parentheses/precedence) instead of always being ANDed - e.g. "temp &#8805; 45C" OR "usage &#8805; 95%" to turn a fan on under either condition.
- Change: the global Connections section is now foldable per-connection, like rules already were, so a long list of saved connections doesn't force endless scrolling.

###2026.08.03.19
- **CONFIG CHANGE**: rules now support multiple conditions per rule, ANDed together (a "+ Add Condition" button in the rule editor) - e.g. "disk temp &#8804; 35C" AND "no parity check/rebuild active", so a temperature-driven fan-off rule doesn't fight a separate "keep the fan on during parity check" rule over the same relay. The old single trigger/disks/aggregate/threshold fields are now the rule's first (and by default only) condition; existing rules load and keep working unchanged, just now editable as a one-condition group. The rule-level "Direction" field is renamed "Action" (Turn ON/Turn OFF) - what fires once every condition is simultaneously true.

###2026.08.03.18
- Add: **Test** button on each global Connection (MQTT/Tasmota HTTP) to verify reachability/auth without needing a rule. Webhook connections have no fixed endpoint of their own, so their Test button is disabled - test those from a rule instead.
- Remove: the per-rule "Test MQTT Connection" button, now redundant with testing the connection directly under Global Settings (a rule with a "Custom" MQTT connection can still use its own "Check Device State" button).

###2026.08.03.17
- Add: global **Connections** - define a named MQTT broker or HTTP/webhook endpoint once under Global Settings, then pick it from any rule's protocol dropdown instead of re-entering host/port/credentials per rule. Rules can still enter connection details manually ("Custom") as before.
- Change: Global Settings and Connections now each have their own Save &amp; Apply button, and each rule has its own Save &amp; Apply button, instead of one button saving every rule and setting at once. Adding/removing/reordering/duplicating a rule still saves immediately.

###2026.08.03.16
- Fix: folding/unfolding a rule (or adding a new one) re-rendered every rule box from the in-memory rules array without first syncing unsaved DOM edits back into it - so editing one rule and then folding/unfolding *any* rule (or clicking "+ Add Rule") silently discarded those edits. Both actions now collect current form values before re-rendering.
- Fix: the rule header's Remove/Duplicate/Move/Test buttons used `float:right`, which has no effect inside the header's flex layout - they just sat in DOM order after the state badge, shifting left/right whenever the badge's text changed length ("Idle" vs "Fired: ON"). Wrapped them in their own right-aligned flex group and gave the badge a fixed minimum width so neither shifts anymore.

###2026.08.03.15
- Fix: parity check/rebuild direction labels said "Fire when it starts"/"Fire when it ends", implying the poller only catches the exact transition moment. It's actually level-triggered - an ON rule fires whenever a poll cycle sees the operation active, so it correctly fires even if the operation was already running before the poller noticed (or before/after a plugin restart). Relabeled to "Turn ON while active"/"Turn OFF while inactive".

###2026.08.03.14
- Change: added a divider between the threshold/delay fields and "Force resend", separating it visually from the trigger condition above.

###2026.08.03.13
- Fix: v2026.08.03.12's own CHANGES text contained an unescaped degree-sign entity reference that isn't a predefined XML entity (only &amp;amp; &amp;lt; &amp;gt; &amp;quot; &amp;apos; and this file's own declared entities are valid) - this broke *installing or updating the plugin at all* ("XML file doesn't exist or xml parse error"). Removed it and added an XML well-formedness check (resolving all entities, not just checking tag balance) to the release process so this class of bug can't ship again.
- Add: **Move Up/Down** buttons on each rule to reorder them - rules already executed in their stored order, but there was no way to actually rearrange that order from the webGUI.

###2026.08.03.12
- **BREAKING CONFIG CHANGE**: a rule now fires exactly one direction instead of a paired ON/OFF (on_temp/off_temp, on_topic/off_topic, on_payload/off_payload, on_url/off_url, etc. are all replaced by a single `direction` + `threshold` + action). Hysteresis (e.g. "on at 40C, off at 35C") is now achieved by pairing two rules - an ON rule and an OFF rule - rather than one rule tracking both thresholds internally. Existing rules created before this update will need to be recreated as separate ON and OFF rules; sorry for the inconvenience, but the old combined shape didn't extend cleanly to the new trigger types below.
- Add: **Duplicate** button on each rule, to quickly clone one (e.g. to create the paired opposite-direction rule for hysteresis, or a similar rule for another device).
- Add: parity check and array/data rebuild are now full trigger types (alongside disk temperature/usage), each with their own ON/OFF rules that fire directly off Unraid's array-operation state - replacing the old "also force ON during parity check/rebuild" checkboxes that only modified temperature rules.
- Change: the rule state badge now shows "Fired: ON/OFF" or "Idle" instead of a device relay state, matching the single-direction model.

###2026.08.03.11
- Change: added dividers and labels in the webhook rule editor clarifying that headers/auth/insecure-cert are shared across ON, OFF, and the state check, and that the state URL/method are only used by "Check Device State" - previously these sat directly below the OFF fields with no visual separation, making them look OFF-specific.

###2026.08.03.10
- Fix: updates silently failed to actually replace the installed files since the very first release, even though Settings &gt; Plugins showed the new version number as "up-to-date". The locally-installed package file was always named the same literal `unraid-disk-event-trigger.txz` with no version in the filename, so Slackware's `upgradepkg` saw an identical package name/version on every release and skipped reinstalling - only the `.plg`'s own version metadata (a separate field) was actually being refreshed. The package is now installed under a properly versioned filename (`unraid-disk-event-trigger-&lt;version&gt;-noarch-1.txz`) so each release is correctly detected and installed. If you're on an older version, this fix itself needs one more manual update/reinstall to take effect (via Check for Updates, or reinstalling with the .plg URL) - after that, updates will apply correctly going forward.

###2026.08.03.9
- Fix: the in-package README.md (which Unraid's Settings &gt; Plugins page reads as the plugin description) was stale, still only describing Tasmota/MQTT temperature triggers - updated to mention the webhook protocol, MQTT TLS, and disk usage % triggers.

###2026.08.03.8
- Add: rules can now trigger on used disk space percentage instead of temperature (per-rule "Trigger on" choice), reusing the same aggregate/hysteresis/delay logic. Usage is read from Unraid's own cached filesystem stats, so it's safe to check even for spun-down disks.
- Change: the disk table now shows a Used % column alongside temperature.

###2026.08.03.7
- Add: rules can now target a generic HTTP/HTTPS webhook (arbitrary method/URL/body/headers per ON/OFF, optional basic auth and a self-signed-certificate bypass) in addition to Tasmota HTTP and MQTT, for devices/automations Tasmota's fixed API doesn't cover.
- Add: webhook rules get a "Check Device State" button that shows the raw HTTP response for debugging, since there's no generic way to infer on/off from an arbitrary endpoint's reply.
- Add: MQTT connections can now use TLS (mqtts), with an option to ignore invalid/self-signed broker certificates on trusted LAN brokers.

###2026.08.03.6
- Change: replaced the icon with a bolder-stroke version so it's actually legible at Unraid's small fixed icon size (the previous thin-outline artwork stayed faint even after cropping/dilation attempts).

###2026.08.03.5
- Change: thickened the icon's line strokes so it reads clearly and looks appropriately bold at Unraid's small fixed icon sizes, not just faint thin outlines.

###2026.08.03.4
- Change: cropped the plugin icon tighter to its artwork (it had noticeable empty margin) so it renders a bit larger within Unraid's fixed-size icon boxes.

###2026.08.03.3
- Fix: icon still missing in two more places. The .plg's `launch` attribute had a bogus nested path with a stray ".page" suffix, and the Settings &gt; Plugins list page reads its icon from a separate `icon="..."` attribute on the &lt;PLUGIN&gt; tag (not the .page file at all) - neither was set correctly. Dropped the earlier (incorrect) Tag/icons/ attempt, which the reference plugin this was checked against doesn't use. Verified this time by directly invoking Unraid's actual icon-resolution code (ShowPlugins.php's logic, and PluginHelpers.php's plugin()/icon()) against the real installed .plg and files, not just reasoning about it.

###2026.08.03.2
- Fix: icon still didn't show on the plugin's own page (only the Settings/Utilities tile picked it up). That header icon is driven by a separate "Tag" attribute, resolved from &lt;plugin&gt;/icons/&lt;tag&gt; - now set. Verified server-side by invoking Unraid's actual icon-resolving functions against the deployed files before releasing, rather than assuming.

###2026.08.03.1
- Fix: custom plugin icon didn't actually show up. The Settings/Utilities tile grid resolves a .png Icon via &lt;plugin&gt;/images/&lt;icon&gt; (falling back to the plugin root), not &lt;plugin&gt;/icons/&lt;icon&gt; like the top-nav utility buttons do - the icon is now shipped in both locations.

###2026.08.03
- Add: custom disk+thermometer plugin icon, shown in the Utilities menu and the Settings/Plugins listing (previously the built-in generic "temperature" icon).

###2026.08.02.12
- Fix: the delay tooltip was a plain `title` on the number input, which is easy to miss. Replaced with a visible (i) info icon next to each delay field.

###2026.08.02.11
- Add: tooltip on each on/off delay field explaining what it does.

###2026.08.02.10
- Change: the on/off delay is now two independent settings - "Turn ON at" and "Turn OFF at" each have their own delay field shown right next to the temperature, instead of one shared delay for both directions.

###2026.08.02.9
- Add: optional per-rule "turn on/off delay" (seconds). When set, a threshold crossing is only sent once the condition has held continuously for that long; if it reverts before then, the pending change is cancelled. Forced-ON during a parity check/rebuild still fires immediately, bypassing the delay.

###2026.08.02.8
- Add: the YAML backup view is now a writable editor - edit and click "Validate &amp; Apply YAML" to save. Both a YAML syntax parser and structural validation run server-side before anything is written; invalid input is rejected with a specific error and never touches the saved config.
- Change: "View as YAML" is now a toggle (View/Hide), and the download filename is unraid-disk-event-trigger.yaml.

###2026.08.02.7
- Fix: plugin description didn't show up on Settings &gt; Plugins. Unraid's native Plugins page (ShowPlugins.php) reads README.md directly off disk from the installed plugin directory - a previous release wrongly excluded it from the package. Now included again.

###2026.08.02.6
- Fix: "tracked state" badge got reset to unknown whenever a rule was folded/unfolded; now cached and preserved across re-renders.
- Change: badge now shows only "State: ON/OFF/unknown", no temperature, with a clearer visual style.
- Add: top-of-page "Plugin enabled" toggle - when off, the background poller process itself is stopped entirely (not just idled), including across reboots.
- Add: "View as YAML" / download for the current config, for easy backup.
- Fixed the package build to not include README.md or macOS AppleDouble metadata junk files.

###2026.08.02.5
- Fix: Check Device State's MQTT read could time out for devices/bridges (e.g. zigbee2mqtt) that don't retain state and only report on change. Now publishes a "&lt;state_topic&gt;/get" trigger before waiting, prompting an immediate report. Verified against a real Sonoff ZBMicro plug via zigbee2mqtt.
- Add: each rule now shows a live "tracked state" badge (ON/OFF/unknown, with last known temp), refreshed automatically and immediately after Check Device State.

###2026.08.02.4
- Fix: a manual Test ON/OFF didn't update the tracked hysteresis state, so a Test OFF after an automatic ON left the poll cycle believing the relay was still on and it never re-fired.
- Add: per-rule "force_resend" flag to re-send the ON/OFF command every poll cycle even when unchanged, as protection against the device drifting out of sync.
- Add: "Check Device State" button (Tasmota HTTP status query, or an MQTT state topic) that reads the device's actual state without changing anything and self-heals the tracked relay state to match.
- Add: MQTT state-topic JSON parsing is an explicit opt-in checkbox (default off) rather than automatic sniffing.

###2026.08.02.3
- Add: "Clear Log" button (with confirmation prompt) next to Refresh Log.

###2026.08.02.2
- Fix: Save &amp; Apply (and every other write action) silently did nothing, because Unraid's webGUI drops any POST missing a csrf_token. Now wired through correctly.
- Add: show/hide toggle on password fields.
- Add: "Test MQTT Connection" button that checks broker reachability and auth without publishing anything.
- Add: rules collapse by default when there's more than one, stay open when there's exactly one; manual toggle overrides.
- Fix: cramped padding on collapsed rules; labeled the per-rule "Enabled" checkbox.
- All changes verified live via the Chrome extension against an authenticated Unraid session, not just simulated.

###2026.08.02.1
- Fix: .plg install script's heredoc broke XML parsing, causing "XML file doesn't exist or xml parse error" during install.
- Fix: srcURL used the un-prefixed version for the release tag, causing a 404 on the .txz download during install.
- Fix: disk temperature table stayed empty on page load/refresh (get_status returned disks as a JSON object instead of an array).
- All three issues found and fixed by installing and exercising the plugin end-to-end on a real Unraid 7.3.2 box.

###2026.08.02
- Initial release: SMART disk temp monitoring with HTTP/MQTT (Tasmota &amp; zigbee2mqtt) triggers, MQTT username/password auth, and optional forced-on triggers during parity checks and array rebuilds.
</CHANGES>

<FILE Name="/boot/config/plugins/&name;/&name;-&version;-noarch-1.txz" Run="upgradepkg --install-new">
<URL>&srcURL;</URL>
<MD5>&srcMD5;</MD5>
</FILE>

<FILE Run="/bin/bash">
<INLINE>
mkdir -p /boot/config/plugins/&name;
# Remove previous release's package file(s), now that the package name is
# properly versioned (name-version-noarch-1.txz) so upgradepkg reliably
# detects and installs each new version instead of silently no-opping on an
# unversioned filename that never changed across releases.
find /boot/config/plugins/&name; -maxdepth 1 -name '&name;-*-noarch-1.txz' ! -name '&name;-&version;-noarch-1.txz' -delete
if [ ! -f /boot/config/plugins/&name;/rules.json ]; then
  printf '%s\n' '{' '    "poll_interval": 60,' '    "rules": []' '}' > /boot/config/plugins/&name;/rules.json
fi
chmod +x /etc/rc.d/rc.unraid-disk-event-trigger
if grep -q '"enabled": *false' /boot/config/plugins/&name;/rules.json 2>/dev/null; then
  echo "Plugin is disabled in saved config, not starting the poller."
else
  /etc/rc.d/rc.unraid-disk-event-trigger restart
fi
echo "unraid-disk-event-trigger installed."
</INLINE>
</FILE>

<FILE Run="/bin/bash" Method="remove">
<INLINE>
/etc/rc.d/rc.unraid-disk-event-trigger stop
removepkg &name;
</INLINE>
</FILE>

</PLUGIN>
